<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harold Macmillan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harold Macmillan was Prime Minister of the United Kingdom from 1957 to 1963. In his 1960 '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wind-of-change-speech">Wind of Change</a>' speech in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> he acknowledged the strength of African nationalism. His government oversaw the rapid independence of many British colonies, a central phase of the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan.jpg" alt="Harold Macmillan" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harold_Macmillan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maurice Harold Macmillan was born on 10 February 1894 in Belgravia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the son of the publisher Maurice Crawford Macmillan and Helen Belles, an American from Spencer, Indiana. His grandfather Daniel MacMillan, who founded Macmillan Publishers, was the son of a Scottish crofter from the Isle of Arran. He was educated at Eton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served in the Grenadier Guards and was wounded three times. At the Battle of Flers-Courcelette in September 1916 he lay for over twelve hours in a shell hole, reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> in Greek, and he suffered pain and partial immobility for the rest of his life. He married Lady Dorothy Cavendish on 21 April 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Macmillan won Stockton-on-Tees in 1924, lost it in 1929 and regained it in 1931 as a supporter of economic planning. He opposed the appeasement of Nazi Germany and first held office in 1940 at the Ministry of Supply. Late in 1942 he became British Minister Resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Under Churchill he became Minister of Housing in 1951, charged with building 300,000 houses a year, then Minister of Defence in 1954. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a> he was Foreign Secretary in 1955 and Chancellor of the Exchequer from December 1955.</p><p>When Eden resigned in 1957 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>, Macmillan succeeded him as prime minister. In July 1957 he told the nation it had never had it so good, but warned of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, and he led the Conservatives to success in 1959 with an increased majority. He worked to rebuild the Special Relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and presided over much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1957, citing the wind of change blowing across the continent. He ended National Service, acquired Polaris and pioneered the Nuclear Test Ban with the United States and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. A French veto blocked the United Kingdom's entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Vassall affair, in which an Admiralty clerk was convicted in October 1962 of passing secrets to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the Profumo affair damaged his government. After a controversial Cabinet reshuffle he resigned the premiership in October 1963, citing ill health, and was succeeded by Lord Home. He stood down from the Commons at the 1964 election, after 37 years of service. He was Chancellor of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> from 1960 until his death and chaired Macmillan Publishers from 1964 to 1974, publishing a multi-volume autobiography. He received the Order of Merit in 1976 and died on 29 December 1986, aged 92.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harold Macmillan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Harold Macmillan</b>'s “Wind of Change” speech in Cape Town in 1960 acknowledged the inevitability of African decolonisation, and the wave of independence that followed, beginning with Ghana in 1957 and Nigeria in 1960, ended Britain's African empire within little more than a decade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chelsea, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Horsted Keynes, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.17&amp;year=1953" aria-label="Chelsea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Harold Macmillan"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4991 5725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4991 5725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.17&amp;year=1953">What was here in 1953? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
